From e41d5bda01cfcd4e615a96d92a4ad35c436b7dbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 23:35:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 63/65] test: add feature_p2mr_tripwire.py

A functional test of P2MR leaf version 0xe0 and its tripwire outputs on
regtest, with three of them (two the test can spend, one locked to the
NUMS point) and six nodes: two with the rules (one with
-acceptnonstdtxn=1), one without them until it upgrades late, a pruned
one, one with other parameters, and one that loads the regtest
assumeutxo snapshot at height 299. It covers:

- the activation boundary: the next block's flags at tips H-2, H-1 and
  H; below H a block takes a 0xe0 spend with an invalid signature and
  the mempool refuses even valid ones, at depth zero both take them; a
  reorg below H empties both mempools of such spends;
- after H, Schnorr and ML-DSA-44 spends of 0xe0 leaves confirm, an
  invalid ML-DSA-44 signature fails in the mempool and in a block, and
  0xe0 leaves follow the policy of 0xc0 leaves, which they do at every
  height and at depth zero too: a spend there with a witness element
  above 80 bytes is refused below H and after it, and a node with
  -acceptnonstdtxn takes it into a valid block both times;
- the block that spends a tripwire output, with Schnorr spends of 0xe0
  leaves before and after the spend, all valid, while the same spends
  fail in the next block, in the mempool and in proposals, past the
  script and signature caches filled before; a spend only in the
  mempool changes nothing;
- the mempool when a block disables the checks: Schnorr spends go with
  their descendants, through unconfirmed parents, a package, TRUC and
  mixed inputs, ML-DSA-44 spends stay; and after a reorg to a branch
  that spends the other tripwire output, a parent that comes back to
  the mempool keeps its ML-DSA-44 child and loses its Schnorr one, and
  getblocktemplate still works;
- a reorganization whose first block repeats the transactions of the
  block it replaces, coinbase included, so that nothing is left to put
  back, and whose next block spends a tripwire output: the node keeps a
  child whose relative lock refers to the replaced block, and drops the
  Schnorr spend;
- invalidateblock and reconsiderblock over the trigger, both outputs
  missing, one of them back, the creation of the outputs moved by a
  reorg to H or above, the NUMS output, an output that never existed,
  and a node whose height of the rules is above the trigger;
- the logs, which name a spend only once its block is in the active
  chain (not for a proposal, an invalid candidate or the background
  chainstate of a snapshot), and gettripwireinfo;
- VerifyDB at level 4, restarts with -reindex and -reindex-chainstate,
  pruning, the snapshot chainstate and its background chainstate, a
  late upgrade over a chain with and without a block that breaks the
  rules, and the options a node refuses to start with, also on the
  chains they are not for.

feature_p2mr_signet.py checks that neither of its signets has the rules
without -signettripwire, and that gettripwireinfo says so.
